   "text": "Does lightning strike all at once?"
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 2.533333333333333,
   "dur": 37.658,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "Beyond the window, a storm. It doesn't. The whole fall from the cloud takes tens of thousandths of a second. At this speed, that would take hours. So I'll skip ahead, and show you the highlights. First, a faint path of electric charge reaches down from the cloud, in jumps. Each jump is tens of meters long. Each takes about a microsecond. Here, one jump lasts one whole second. Then the path waits, about fifty microseconds. Here, that's almost a minute. Look at how it branches, like a tree turned upside down."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 43.3,
   "dur": 24.326,
